WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740
On the magnitude scale, a smaller number means a

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:06.280
brighter object. A difference of five magnitudes corresponds to

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.600
a brightness factor of one hundred. Watch brightness fall

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.640
as the magnitude number rises. Absolute magnitude means apparent

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.720
magnitude at ten parsecs, with extinction removed. Moving the

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.380
same star from ten to one hundred parsecs makes

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.400
it one hundred times fainter and increases its apparent

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.050
magnitude by five. Dust also makes a star fainter.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.780
If you attribute all that dimming to distance, you

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:35.820
overestimate the distance. Keep intrinsic luminosity, distance, and extinction

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.808
separate when interpreting apparent magnitude.
